WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.433
Modern Editor brings your source, PDF, and review comments into one place. Open a paper and

00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:07.218
start writing.

00:00:07.817 --> 00:00:12.845
Choose a review template, or give your own instructions. Here, we ask for grammar and

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:17.874
clarity, with the smallest local edits. New comments can join the queue as they arrive.

00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:24.192
Read the reason, then see the changes in their paragraph. Switch to Clean for the proposed

00:00:24.192 --> 00:00:26.107
wording, or Edit to adjust it.

00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:32.107
Want another wording? Quick alternative adds a saved choice. Switch between versions, or

00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:37.696
edit one yourself. Asking for an alternative leaves both the source and your selected

00:00:37.696 --> 00:00:38.494
wording alone.

00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:45.232
Preview compiles a tentative draft and marks the nearby passage in the PDF. Inspect how the

00:00:45.232 --> 00:00:49.253
suggestion reads on the page, then return to your unchanged draft.

00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:55.826
Use Shift A to accept and move forward. These three corrections go straight into the draft,

00:00:55.826 --> 00:00:58.411
without waiting for compilation after each one.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:04.857
Compile once to refresh the PDF from the current draft. Acceptance and compilation are

00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:05.701
separate actions.

00:01:06.217 --> 00:01:11.773
For an edit you want to check immediately, choose Accept and compile. The editor checks the

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:16.634
proposed draft, applies it, and waits while you inspect the PDF. Then choose Next.

00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:23.571
Codex Side Chat can help with the substance of the paper. Here, we ask why the optimum is

00:01:23.571 --> 00:01:28.539
unique and whether the distribution matters. The answer refers to the current draft, while

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:29.959
the manuscript stays unchanged.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:35.559
Switch to editor help for questions about the application. The conversation makes its scope

00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:40.468
visible, and the response explains the controls. You can return to the paper conversation

00:01:40.468 --> 00:01:41.871
whenever you need it.

00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:48.206
A useful answer can become a review comment. Here, a proposed clarification returns to the

00:01:48.206 --> 00:01:54.028
usual queue, where you can edit, preview, accept, or reject it. Chatting alone never applies

00:01:54.028 --> 00:01:54.804
the change.

00:01:55.317 --> 00:02:01.667
Classic view offers a cream writing surface and a compact suggestion panel. It uses the same

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:05.635
draft, saving, and review workflow. Switch back whenever you prefer.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:12.474
Switch the right-hand PDF pane to Changes PDF. All three columns stay in place. Generation

00:02:12.474 --> 00:02:15.004
takes time; this wait is shortened.

00:02:15.517 --> 00:02:21.349
This is the LaTeX diff view. Deleted wording is struck through, and new wording is

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:26.404
underlined. Step through the changes in the typeset paper, keeping equations and surrounding

00:02:26.404 --> 00:02:31.069
prose in view. It is a separate comparison; your manuscript stays clean.

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:36.820
Press Why to see the recorded reason for a change. The explanation opens below the paper,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.420
with the exact source comparison tucked away until you need it.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:46.380
Switch to Clean paper to read the revised wording without deletion markup, using the same

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.091
captured comparison.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:53.757
Text diff shows the exact LaTeX changes. It is also the place to inspect differences that

00:02:53.757 --> 00:02:56.047
the PDF comparison cannot safely display.

00:02:56.517 --> 00:02:59.563
Save your draft, close the project, and return home.
